## Pitfalls

- **Clipping bias.** A hard per-pixel clip removes faint emission and biases moment 0 low and moment 1 toward
  bright gas. Masking "removes faint diffuse emission" (2013ApJ...769L..12L). Use a smoothed or
  seed-and-grow mask, and compare total flux with an unclipped sum. Clipping negatives only biases high.
- **Missing flux.** Interferometer-only moment 0 underestimates extended emission: >6 arcsec structures in
  2013AJ....146...47I, >9.1 arcsec in 2014AJ....147..141H, lower-limit masses in 2016MNRAS.460..627K and
  2021A&A...648A..41V. Feathered data gave a 20-50% higher streamer mass in 2025ApJ...985..166K; dropping
  baselines under 60 m lost 9% of the flux in 2025A&A...703A..15M, which scaled it back. Compare with
  single-dish flux where you can (2019PASJ...71...85M, 2017A&A...608A.134Y against IRAM 30m).
- **Foreground absorption.** Cloud absorption removed about 30% of 12CO on one side of AS 209
  (2018ApJ...868..113T) and affects 12CO near systemic or redshifted velocities in almost all the Taurus
  multiple-system discs, with the southern half of DH Tau A fully absorbed (2022A&A...662A.121R).
  2024NatAs...8..587F doubled the blue side to correct red-shifted absorption (an assumption, not a
  measurement). Mask or flag cloud-velocity channels (2021A&A...648A..41V).
- **Moment 1 on multi-component lines.** A single centroid averages components; papers switch to two- or
  multi-Gaussian fits (2013AJ....146...47I, 2018ApJ...865...13T, 2015ApJ...803...97S).
- **Moment 2 is not turbulence.** Beam smearing and rotation inflate it (2019MNRAS.487..444R,
  2025A&A...699A..83A); resolution limits velocity-shear modelling (2016NatAs...1E...3P); map-based
  rotation estimates can be biased by beam smearing (2021A&A...654A..37D).
- **Imaging artefacts.** A central [CII] dip was a cleaning artefact (2024A&A...686A.156D); 12-m-only data
  may hold artefacts at low velocities (2025ApJ...985..166K); JvM-corrected maps can understate the rms
  (2024ApJ...964..190L).
- **Continuum subtraction errors** leak into moment 0 near bright continuum and at band edges
  (2017A&A...606A.125S: N2D+ "on the edge of a strong atmospheric feature").
- **Blends.** Severe blends block a spatial analysis (2018A&A...615A.155H); papers map only unblended
  transitions (2024MNRAS.533.1143M) or split shared flux by assumption (halved in 2021A&A...651L...5V).
- **Keplerian mask circularity.** Masks built from assumed M*, i, PA bias weak-line maps toward the model;
  2016ApJ...832..204Y notes the gain depends on an accurate Keplerian velocity and drops inside the beam.
- **Unstated settings.** Cards flag clipping thresholds (18), primary beam correction (23), CASA version
  (54, or 104 with combined phrasings) and line-free channels (~227) as not stated (pack aggregates). Do not
  infer them; ask or say so.
- **Optical depth.** Thick lines (12CO) trace temperature and surface, not column (2021A&A...648A..19W;
  C18O brightness is only a lower limit on gas temperature in 2024ApJ...964..190L). Masses from an
  optically thin conversion applied to opaque emission are lower limits (2026A&A...705A.198M for CO in
  debris disks; 2014ApJ...783L..13P makes the same point for continuum vortex masses). Empirical
  alpha_CO / X_CO conversions are a different case: the factor itself is uncertain and may overestimate the
  mass (2025ApJ...988...72S, 2024ApJ...963....1O, 2018ApJ...865...13T), so quote that uncertainty separately.

## Verification checks

What the papers did:
- Vary the velocity window and confirm sizes and positions hold (2024A&A...687A.163B), or pick the window that
  maximises S/N and confirm by eye (2020MNRAS.493.4294B). Vary the mask smoothing and check the spatial and
  velocity coherence of what survives (2022MNRAS.509.2920N).
- Compare total flux with single-dish or other interferometers (2019PASJ...71...85M, 2014A&A...568A..92M,
  2018ApJ...853..147M); apply the same aperture to different array combinations and check the flux is
  recovered (2024A&A...686A.156D).
- For fitted line maps, check recovered flux against the channel sum (about 97% in the worst cases in
  2021ApJ...908..231M).
- Re-run without the JvM correction (2024ApJ...974..102S) and compare velocity maps from different estimators
  (moment 1, Gaussian, quadratic: same paper).
- Check the PA or morphology across lines or transitions (2014ApJ...784...78R across CO(1-0) and CO(3-2);
  2019ApJ...871..112X across upper-level energies; 2016A&A...592A..76D positions within 8 mas across lines);
  compare line-map PA and inclination with the continuum (2025A&A...703A..15M, 2022A&A...662A.121R).
- Compare the modelled velocity field with the observed moment 1 (2020ApJ...890..167T); for a Keplerian fit
  to high-velocity channel maps, inspect residual channel maps and compare the fitted PA and inclination with
  the continuum (2018ApJ...869...59W); vary the inner fit radius to test residuals (2024MNRAS.534.2904C).
- Apply the same mask and velocity range to model cubes as to the data (2024A&A...687A.311A, 2024ApJ...972...88L).
- Confirm a moment-1 gradient in a PV diagram before calling it rotation (not confirmed for DC8187(W) in
  2024A&A...686A.156D); cross-check with a second fitter (2024A&A...691A.118K) or an independent profile
  (surface temperatures against peak-intensity profiles in 2024ApJ...964..190L).

What a careful referee adds:
- Overlay the mask on channel maps and show that no emission falls outside it.
- Report moment 0 flux with and without clipping, and the rms map, not only a single rms value.
- State whether the moment maps and fluxes are primary-beam corrected and give the beam for every map.
- For velocity fields, show the S/N cut, what it is applied to, and how many pixels survive.

